The sandbox (the threat model in one paragraph). Evidence commands execute code from the tree under review — untrusted code — so they run confined: in a throwaway copy of the tree (`.git` excluded, deleted afterwards, so a hostile command cannot rewrite the checkout you are about to merge), with an allow-listed environment — your provider API keys, tokens, and SSH agent never reach an evidence process, and HOME/TMPDIR point inside the sandbox so credential files are unreachable through them; with no network by default (proxy variables stripped, and a Linux network namespace where unshare is available — the record states which isolation actually applied, and an item must opt in with "allow_network": true to run unrestricted); and under resource limits (wall-clock timeout enforced by killing the process group, CPU and per-file-size rlimits). Every item's record carries its exit code, duration, and the sha256 of its full output; only a redacted tail is stored, so a scanner that prints the secret it found cannot smuggle it into the record. Commands and adapter choices come from you (the config you point the CLI at), never from the diff.